ding one Tilt R call — and a single dated, partisan-sponsored poll showing Merrin ahead keep this well short of Likely D.", "watch": "Fresh general-election polling this fall or a late national environment shift toward either party could move this into outright Tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Marcy Kaptur (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Marcy Kaptur, one of the longest-serving members of the House, seeks another term in a northern Ohio district that redistricting redrew with a five-point Republican lean. She faces Republican Derek Merrin in a rematch, with Libertarian Matthew Althaus also on the ballot. Kaptur holds a large financial edge, having raised $4.3 million and banking $3.5 million, while Merrin has raised $1.5 million with $531,000 on hand. Betting markets put her chances near 72 percent.\n\nExpert ratings split between Tossup and Lean D, with Inside Elections alone calling the race Tilt R for a flip. Polling is sparse: an April survey from a Republican-affiliated firm showed Merrin up four, and little has been published since. Headlines show Kaptur touting federal grid awards and distancing herself from her party on some issues, while Merrin appears with Vivek Ramaswamy and presses attacks on tariffs, jobs, and living costs. The race leans Democratic but remains genuinely competitive.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7474, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7282, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7313, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7108, "margin": 3.0}]}